/* shared/creatures.js — 0.2.0 — the Pokémon a person is shown as.

   Moved out of CHECK IN on 2026-09-22 so COACH's box and CHECK IN draw the
   same creature for the same client from one table. Tom asked for Pokémon in
   place of CHECK IN's animals, and was told the art is Nintendo's and the
   suite is public: "it's fine".

   2026-09-23, DOT ART. The first version was ten Pokémon drawn from circles
   and curves, and they looked like circles and curves. Tom pointed at a page
   of bead patterns — every Pokémon as a grid of flat squares — and said to
   pull the Pokémon from it. So all 151 of the first generation are here as
   pixel art, read off those grids cell by cell.

   Still nothing downloaded and still no image files. A sprite is two strings:
   its own palette, six hex characters a colour run together, and the picture,
   run-length coded over that palette. One shared colour table was tried first
   and thrown away — 921 distinct colours across the 151, and squashing them
   into one table close enough to index with a single letter meant flattening
   the shading that tells one orange Pokémon from another.

   Each sprite is painted once into a tiny canvas at one pixel per cell and
   kept, then blown up with smoothing off. That last part is the whole trick:
   it is what keeps the squares square at any size.

   Like notice.js and range.js, this writes colours down: a creature is part of
   a picture that leaves the app, so it is the same creature in every theme.

   A kind this file does not know (an old animal pick: bear, cat...) is drawn
   as the Pokémon LEGACY maps it to, and anything else as Pikachu, so an old
   row never draws nothing.                                                 */
